# Sema

Name resolution, type checking, the semantic checks, and the lowerings that
reduce a checked tree to the core Kairo Codegen emits. The compiler builds
itself through this Sema; `--type-check-only` stops after it and is the fast
loop when a change does not touch lowering or codegen.

The specification, read before changing anything here: Compiler/Spec/
(index in Compiler/Spec/README.md). Pass order and invariants are in
Compiler/Spec/Pipeline.md; imports in Compiler/Spec/Imports.md; the contract
between Lower/ and Codegen in Compiler/Spec/Lowering.md and
Compiler/Spec/Codegen.md.

Layout:
- Sema.k, SemaContext.k, SemaDump.k  driver, shared state, --print-sema
- Resolve/  name, member, type and import resolution, redecl merge, chain
            binding, access checks
- Type/     ExprTyper and its parts: calls, overload resolution, argument
            deduction, conversions, casts, literals, operators, patterns
- Check/    semantic checks: class lifecycle, conformance, init / move,
            control flow, returns, patterns, effects (ConstChecking.k and
            LayoutPass.k are stubs)
- Verify/   structural checks between expansion and name resolution (arm
            order, labels, loop context, pattern bindings, C++ names,
            module pins)
- Expand/   eval-if folding, primitive-limit / alloc / requires desugaring
            (MacroExpansion.k is a stub and is not scheduled)
- Foreign/  C++ (ffi) decls in and out: header index, decl/type import
            and export, foreign instantiation, the C++ body walk MoveCheck
            asks whether a C++ function moves out of a reference parameter
- Incremental/  incremental Sema (Compiler/Spec/IncrementalSema.md):
            declaration identities, interface hashes and dependency rows
            (Facts), the invalidation rules (Plan), taking re-checked files
            out of the instantiation registry for a live check (Retract)
- Lower/    one pass per construct lowered away (match, nullable, panic,
            finally, operators, iteration, yield, ...)

## Design notes

- Import folding: phase I folds each import's source symbol table into
  the TU's import overlay (Resolve/ImportResolution.k,
  AST/Context/ImportOverlay.k). After it the TU is self-contained: later
  stages only read other TUs, and the only cross-TU writes go to the two
  locked build-wide stores (canonical types, the instantiation registry).
  That is what lets the fronts of import groups that do not import each
  other run in parallel.
- Class lifecycle: Check/ClassLifecycle.k decides copyable / movable /
  trivial / destructible per record.
- Virtual destructor rule: a class deriving a POLYMORPHIC base (one with a
  virtual method) whose destructor is not virtual, when the derived class
  has a non-trivial destructor, is SC056E. A base with no virtual method (a
  mixin) is exempt, as in clang's -Wdelete-non-virtual-dtor
  (also in ClassLifecycle.k). Without it, deleting a Derived through a Base
  runs only Base's destructor:

    class Base    { fn op finally (self) { ... } }
    class Derived derives Base { fn op finally (self) { ... } }

  The fix is `virtual fn op finally` on Base and `override` on Derived.
- Value categories (lvalue / xvalue / prvalue): assigned inline by
  ExprTyper, since overload resolution needs them while the call is still
  being typed; Check/ValueCategoryPass.k only verifies none is left Unknown.
- Mono: every use of a generic at concrete arguments registers an instance
  (AST/Context/InstantiationRegistry.k); the instance has one home TU that
  emits the specialization, and every other TU using it emits
  `extern template` (Compiler/Spec/Codegen.md). A generic body is checked once, against
  its bounds, never per instance; per-instance facts (layout, lifecycle,
  rows, panic sets) are computed by substitution into the bounds and
  pinned or selected in C++ (Compiler/Spec/Generics.md#decisions).

## Open

See Compiler/Spec/Pipeline.md#remaining-work. Notably: ConstChecking and
LayoutPass are stubs and are not scheduled, MacroExpansion (E) is a stub,
and ExtensionOrphanCheck does not exist.
